{"cwd": "/sandbox/runs/harbor-dispatch/runs/eval-INC-86-r1-instincts/harbor-dispatch", "isSidechain": false, "message": {"content": [{"text": "Done! I've successfully resolved **INC-86**. Here's what was fixed:\n\n## Summary\n\n**Problem**: The dangerous-goods stowage check was not detecting conflicts between explosives and oxidizers (or other hazard classes with divisions). This happened because the segregation lookup was only checking primary classes (e.g., \"5\" for oxidizers) but the segregation table stores entries with divisions (e.g., \"5.1\").\n\n**Solution**: Modified the `mustSegregate` function in `src/cargo/hazmat.ts` to:\n1. Check the full class:division format first (e.g., \"5.1\")\n2. Fall back to primary class lookup for backward compatibility\n3. Apply the same logic to both lookups\n\n**Testing**: \n- Added 6 new test cases covering all acceptance criteria\n- All 27 tests pass, including the new ones that verify:\n  - ✅ `mustSegregate(\"1\", \"5.1\")` = true (explosives ↔ oxidizers)\n  - ✅ `mustSegregate(\"5.1\", \"1\")` = true (bidirectional)\n  - ✅ `mustSegregate(\"2.1\", \"5.1\")` = true (flammable gas ↔ oxidizers)\n  - ✅ `mustSegregate(\"5.2\", \"3\")` = true (organic peroxide ↔ flammable liquid)\n  - ✅ `mustSegregate(\"3\", \"8\")` = false (compatible classes stay compatible)\n  - ✅ `mustSegregate(\"8\", \"5.1\")` = false (corrosive ↔ oxidizers compatible)\n\nCommit: `507ff90` — Fix hazmat segregation check for classes with divisions (INC-86)", "type": "text"}], "id": "msg_011CfV9CLxmhtDEmC396v89x", "model": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001", "role": "assistant"}, "sessionId": "9cd90b78-28ee-4764-95bf-68c08ddddabc", "timestamp": "2026-09-28T03:39:36.872Z", "type": "assistant"}
